## Preliminary observation on the statement

As literally written, $\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n}\cos\!\left(\frac{k\pi}{2n}\right)$ is a series in $k$ with $n$ a free parameter. Its general term $\frac{1}{n}\cos\!\left(\frac{k\pi}{2n}\right)$ does **not** tend to $0$ as $k\to\infty$ (it oscillates with fixed amplitude $1/n$), so the series **diverges** and cannot equal $\frac{2}{\pi}$.

The value $\frac{2}{\pi}$ is the well‑known value of the **limit of Riemann sums**

$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\frac{1}{n}\cos\!\left(\frac{k\pi}{2n}\right),$$

which is clearly the intended problem (the "$\infty$" is the limit $n\to\infty$, and the upper limit of the sum is $n$, not $\infty$). I prove that this limit equals $\frac{2}{\pi}$.

## Proof that $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\frac{1}{n}\cos\!\left(\frac{k\pi}{2n}\right)=\frac{2}{\pi}$

Set $\Delta x=\dfrac{\pi}{2n}$, so that $\dfrac{1}{n}=\dfrac{2}{\pi}\,\Delta x$. Then

$$\sum_{k=1}^{n}\frac{1}{n}\cos\!\left(\frac{k\pi}{2n}\right)=\frac{2}{\pi}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\cos\!\left(\frac{k\pi}{2n}\right)\Delta x=\frac{2}{\pi}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\cos(x_k)\,\Delta x,$$

where $x_k=\dfrac{k\pi}{2n}$ are the **right endpoints** of the partition of $\left[0,\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right]$ into $n$ equal subintervals of width $\Delta x=\dfrac{\pi}{2n}$:

$$0=\underbrace{x_0}_{\text{not used}}<x_1=\frac{\pi}{2n}<x_2=\frac{2\pi}{2n}<\cdots<x_n=\frac{\pi}{2}.$$

The sum $\displaystyle\sum_{k=1}^{n}\cos(x_k)\,\Delta x$ is the **right Riemann sum** of the continuous function $f(x)=\cos x$ on $\left[0,\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right]$. Since $\cos x$ is continuous on this closed interval, it is Riemann integrable, and every sequence of Riemann sums whose mesh $\|\mathcal{P}_n\|=\Delta x=\dfrac{\pi}{2n}\to 0$ converges to the integral:

$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\cos(x_k)\,\Delta x=\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\cos x\,dx=\Big[\sin x\Big]_{0}^{\pi/2}=\sin\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)-\sin 0=1-0=1.$$

Therefore

$$\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\frac{1}{n}\cos\!\left(\frac{k\pi}{2n}\right)=\frac{2}{\pi}\cdot 1=\frac{2}{\pi}.$$

## Verification of the Riemann‑sum hypotheses

All conditions for convergence of Riemann sums are satisfied:

1. **Integrability.** $f(x)=\cos x$ is continuous on $\left[0,\dfrac{\pi}{2}\right]$, hence Riemann integrable.
2. **Mesh tending to zero.** $\|\mathcal{P}_n\|=\dfrac{\pi}{2n}\to 0$ as $n\to\infty$.
3. **Tagged points inside subintervals.** The right endpoint $x_k$ of the $k$‑th subinterval $\left[\dfrac{(k-1)\pi}{2n},\dfrac{k\pi}{2n}\right]$ is a valid tag.

By the standard theorem on convergence of Riemann sums for integrable functions, the limit equals the integral regardless of the choice of tags, so the right‑endpoint choice is legitimate.

## Quantitative error bound (optional rigor)

For a $C^1$ function, the right Riemann sum satisfies

$$\left|\sum_{k=1}^{n}f(x_k)\,\Delta x-\int_{0}^{\pi/2}f(x)\,dx\right|\le \frac{\|\mathcal{P}_n\|}{2}\int_{0}^{\pi/2}|f'(x)|\,dx=\frac{\pi}{4n}\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\sin x\,dx=\frac{\pi}{4n}\cdot 1=\frac{\pi}{4n}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}0,$$

confirming convergence to the integral, and hence the original limit to $\frac{2}{\pi}$, at rate $O(1/n)$.

## Conclusion

The literal infinite series $\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n}\cos\!\left(\frac{k\pi}{2n}\right)$ **diverges** (its terms do not tend to $0$) and therefore does **not** equal $\frac{2}{\pi}$. However, the intended object — the limit of Riemann sums $\displaystyle\lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}\frac{1}{n}\cos\!\left(\frac{k\pi}{2n}\right)$ — **does** equal $\frac{2}{\pi}$, as it is exactly $\frac{2}{\pi}$ times the right Riemann sum of $\cos x$ on $\left[0,\frac{\pi}{2}\right]$, which converges to $\frac{2}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\pi/2}\cos x\,dx=\frac{2}{\pi}\cdot 1=\frac{2}{\pi}$.

